GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/health


package com.agrinexus.backend.config;

import org.springframework.boot.health.contributor.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.health.contributor.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.data.mongodb.core.MongoTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component("mongo")
public class MongoHealthConfig implements HealthIndicator {

    private final MongoTemplate mongoTemplate;

    public MongoHealthConfig(MongoTemplate mongoTemplate) {
        this.mongoTemplate = mongoTemplate;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        try {
            mongoTemplate.getDb().runCommand(
                new org.bson.Document("ping", 1)
            );

            return Health.up()
                    .withDetail("database", mongoTemplate.getDb().getName())
                    .withDetail("message", "MongoDB connection is healthy")
                    .build();

        } catch (Exception e) {
            return Health.down()
                    .withDetail("database", mongoTemplate.getDb().getName())
                    .withDetail("error", e.getMessage())
                    .build();
        }
    }
}



//remember it
MongoDB Health Check — Simple Summary

Your MongoDB is working correctly. The problem is only with Spring Boot's health check.

Spring Boot health check sends a hello command to MongoDB's local database. Your Atlas user does not have permission for that operation, so health becomes DOWN. Your actual application database agrinexus_dev is working fine.

Why not give local permission?

local, admin, and config are internal/protected MongoDB databases. MongoDB Atlas recommends avoiding writes to these databases. Also, normal Atlas roles such as readWriteAnyDatabase and dbAdminAnyDatabase specifically exclude local and config.

So:

Your app database:
agrinexus_dev       ✅

Internal database:
local               🔒
If you want to learn Atlas permissions

In Atlas:

Database & Network Access → Database Users → Custom Roles → Add New Custom Role

Atlas allows you to create custom roles and select specific actions + databases/collections.

For example, for your application you could create a role like:

Role name: agrinexusAppRole

Database: agrinexus_dev

Permissions:
    find
    insert
    update
    remove

Then assign this role to:

agrinexus_admin

This is the proper Atlas RBAC approach: give the user only the permissions it actually needs.

But for local

Don't try to give your application user broad access to local.

If your goal is simply:

/actuator/health
        ↓
MongoDB UP / DOWN

then the better solution is to make the health check test your agrinexus_dev database, instead of trying to give your application user special access to MongoDB's internal local database.

Remember this rule 🧠

Application user → application database (agrinexus_dev)
Monitoring/health → check application database
Internal DB (local, config, admin) → don't give unnecessary permissions

That's also the safer/least-privilege approach recommended for Atlas.